So far, we’ve built up the idea of a limit and used it to talk about continuity — what it means for a function to have no gaps, jumps, or breaks. Now we’re ready to put limits to work on the central idea of this course: the derivative.
Here’s the question that motivates everything in this unit. Suppose you know how far something has traveled over some interval of time. Can you say how fast it was going at a single, specific instant? At first this might sound like a strange question — speed seems like something that only makes sense over a stretch of time, since you need a change in distance and a change in time to compute it. But it’s a question we answer informally all the time: a speedometer reading, a radar gun reading, “the car was going 60 mph when it hit the guardrail.” All of these are claims about speed at one instant, not over an interval.
Let’s dig into a concrete example and see what we can figure out.

